ttychatter.mailbot walkthrough
==============================

This walkthrough assumes:

    mailbot user:        $user
    mailbot binary:      /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot
    ttychatter binary:   /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.python
    config file:         /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config
    manpage:             /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot.1
    session directory:   /home/$user/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/sessions
    model cache:         /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/models-cache.json

Install files
-------------

    install -m 0755 ttychatter.mailbot /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot
    install -m 0644 ttychatter.mailbot.1 /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot.1

For a system manpage install:

    doas mkdir -p /usr/local/share/man/man1
    doas install -m 0644 ttychatter.mailbot.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/ttychatter.mailbot.1

Minimum private config
----------------------

Create /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config:

    IMAP_HOST=localhost
    IMAP_PORT=993
    IMAP_SSL=1
    IMAP_USER=$user
    IMAP_MAILBOX=INBOX

    SEND_METHOD=sendmail
    SENDMAIL=/usr/sbin/sendmail

    MAIL_FROM=ttychatter@localhost
    ALLOWED_FROM=you@example.org

    SUBJECT_PREFIX=tc
    SUBJECT_SEPARATOR=:
    SESSION_OPEN_DELIM={
    SESSION_CLOSE_DELIM=}
    CONFIG_SUBJECT_TOKEN=CONFIG
    MODELS_SUBJECT_TOKEN=MODELS
    COMMAND_SUBJECT_TOKEN=CMD
    MAILBOT_SUBJECT_TOKEN=MAILBOT
    BODY_COMMAND_SUBJECTS=MAILBOT,CMD

    TTYCHATTER_COMMAND=/usr/local/bin/python3 /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.python
    SESSION_DIR=/home/$user/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/sessions
    ATTACHMENT_DIR=/home/$user/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/attachments
    MODEL_CACHE_FILE=/home/$user/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/models-cache.json
    MODEL_SEARCH_MAX_MATCHES=100

    MAILBOT_MANPAGE_FILE=/home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot.1

Keep it private:

    chmod 600 /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config

Password source
---------------

Plaintext local password, protected by file mode:

    IMAP_PASSWORD=your-imap-password

Encrypted password file:

    /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot --set-imap-password --gpg

Then the config should contain:

    IMAP_PASSWORD_GPG_FILE=/home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/imap-password.gpg

A cron or daemon process can decrypt that file only if gpg-agent can unlock it
without an interactive prompt. If not, use a protected plaintext IMAP_PASSWORD
line, TTYCHATTER_IMAP_PASSWORD in the service environment, or a local secret
manager appropriate to the host.

Initial diagnostics
-------------------

Run as the mailbot user, not root:

    su -l $user
    /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot --config-file /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config --config
    /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot --config-file /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config --doctor
    /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot --config-file /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config --list-candidates
    /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot --config-file /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config --once --dry-run

If --config shows an incomplete config, make the path explicit with
--config-file or set:

    TTYCHATTER_MAILBOT_CONFIG=/home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config

Cron on OpenBSD
---------------

Install the crontab for $user, not root:

    crontab -u $user -e

Example:

    SHELL=/bin/sh
    HOME=/home/$user
    PATH=/home/$user/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin
    MAILTO=""

    */2 * * * * -s /bin/sh -c 'echo "----- mailbot cron start -----"; /bin/date; /usr/local/bin/python3 /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot --config-file /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config --once; rc=$?; echo "----- mailbot cron end rc=$rc -----"; exit $rc' >>/home/$user/.local/share/ttychatter/mailbot.log 2>&1

Use /usr/local/bin/python3 explicitly if the script shebang is
#!/usr/bin/env python3 and cron has a sparse PATH.

Private-mode mail examples
--------------------------

Chat with a named session:

    Subject: tc:{worklog}

    Summarize where we left off.

Start a new unnamed chat using ttychatter.python's own timestamped name:

    Subject: tc:{new}

    Draft a short note about the build.

List sessions:

    Subject: tc:sessions

Search sessions:

    Subject: tc:search cron

Delete a session by exact sanitized name:

    Subject: tc:delete{old-session}

Use the body-command envelope:

    Subject: tc:CMD

    model-search claude

Use the MAILBOT body-command subject:

    Subject: MAILBOT

    sessions

Use MAILBOT as an AI prompt instead of a command:

    Subject: MAILBOT

    Explain the attached config. Do not modify anything.

Because the first nonempty body line is not a known mailbot command, that body
is treated as a chat prompt.

Model cache commands
--------------------

Send the existing cache only, without refreshing:

    Subject: tc:MODELS

Search the existing cache only:

    Subject: tc:MODELS claude

or:

    Subject: tc:model-search claude

Refresh explicitly:

    Subject: tc:update-models

Do not use tc:MODELS when you mean update-models. The two operations are now
separate on purpose.

Output delivery config
----------------------

Default output settings:

    COMMAND_OUTPUT_MODE=auto
    MODEL_CACHE_OUTPUT=auto
    MODEL_SEARCH_OUTPUT=body
    MODEL_LIST_OUTPUT=auto
    SESSIONS_OUTPUT=body
    CONFIG_OUTPUT=attachment
    OUTPUT_INLINE_MAX_BYTES=50000

Set MODEL_CACHE_OUTPUT=body when you want the cache text inline. Set
MODEL_CACHE_OUTPUT=attachment when you always want models-cache.json attached.
The auto policy uses the body for short readable results and attachments for
large or structured results.

Public demo mode
----------------

For a public demo mailbox, add:

    OPEN_TO_PUBLIC=1
    PUBLIC_SESSION_MODE=per-sender
    PUBLIC_SESSION_PREFIX=public
    PUBLIC_ALLOW_NAMED_SESSIONS=0
    PUBLIC_ALLOWED_COMMANDS=help,manpage,model-cache,model-search,archive-session
    RE_SESSION_SUBJECTS=public

Recommended public cleanup:

    PUBLIC_SESSION_CLEANUP=1
    PUBLIC_SESSION_IDLE_HOURS=72
    PUBLIC_SESSION_TTL_HOURS=168
    PUBLIC_SESSION_MAX_COUNT=200
    PUBLIC_SESSION_MAX_TURNS=12

Recommended public archive behavior:

    PUBLIC_SESSION_INDEX_FILE=/home/$user/.local/state/ttychatter/mailbot/public-sessions.json
    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_ON_DELETE=1
    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_USER_TOGGLE=1
    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_FAILURE_POLICY=keep
    PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_MAX_BYTES=1048576

Run public mode from cron by either setting OPEN_TO_PUBLIC=1 in the config or
adding --open-to-public to the cron command.

Public user behavior
--------------------

A stranger can send:

    Subject: hello

    What can this bot do?

The mailbot treats that as a prompt. The public sender does not need tc:.
The reply subject contains the real public session name, for example:

    Re: public-85956a993804cda1

If the user replies to that subject, the same session resumes.

Public users can use only PUBLIC_ALLOWED_COMMANDS. With the recommended public
allowlist, they may send:

    Subject: tc:help
    Subject: tc:manpage
    Subject: tc:model-cache
    Subject: tc:model-search qwen
    Subject: tc:archive-session status
    Subject: tc:archive-session off
    Subject: tc:archive-session on

They cannot edit config, update the model cache, rename/delete sessions, or run
tty-set unless the operator deliberately adds those dangerous commands to
PUBLIC_ALLOWED_COMMANDS.

Public cleanup and archive
--------------------------

Cleanup runs at the start of --once and --daemon polling. It can also be run
locally:

    /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot --config-file /home/$user/.config/ttychatter/mailbot/config --open-to-public --cleanup-public-sessions --dry-run

The default policy means:

    idle>72h       delete inactive public logs
    ttl>168h       delete old public logs even if occasionally active
    count>200      keep only the newest 200 public logs
    max_turns=12   trim surviving logs to the last 12 user/assistant cycles

If PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_ON_DELETE=1, the bot emails the log to the recorded
public sender before deleting it. With PUBLIC_SESSION_ARCHIVE_FAILURE_POLICY=keep,
a failed archive send keeps the local log. With delete, cleanup deletes anyway.

Config editing by email
-----------------------

Request current config:

    Subject: tc:CONFIG

The bot replies with an attached config file. Edit that attachment and send it
back with the same subject. Plaintext IMAP_PASSWORD and SMTP_PASSWORD values are
redacted from exported config. Password file paths are preserved.

In public mode, tc:CONFIG remains privileged. Non-ALLOWED_FROM public senders
receive a refusal.

Troubleshooting
---------------

If cron logs contain:

    env: python3: No such file or directory

use an absolute Python path in cron and in TTYCHATTER_COMMAND:

    /usr/local/bin/python3 /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.mailbot ...
    TTYCHATTER_COMMAND=/usr/local/bin/python3 /home/$user/bin/ttychatter.python

If root sees an empty config, do not debug as root unless the bot is intended to
run as root. The mailbot reads paths under the process user's HOME unless
--config-file or TTYCHATTER_MAILBOT_CONFIG says otherwise.
